    "body": "American Samoa is a US territory covering seven South Pacific islands and atolls. ( Stock image of healthy coral reef in American Samoa The Ocean Agency. )\nPosted By: Cecilia Jamasmie November 20, 2026\nThe United States government pressed ahead with its first offshore critical minerals auction on Thursday, drawing US$6 million (C$8.6 million) in winning bids despite local opposition and a court setback earlier in the week.\nDeep-sea mining startup Wetstone US Ventures offered the minimum US$3 million for each of two proposed 20-year leases covering about 127,500 sq. km. of seabed off American Samoa . It was the only bidder to submit offers, although another participant had registered. The bids require review before leases can be issued.\n“We had hoped it would never come to this,” Sabrina Suluai-Mahuka, founder of American Samoan environmental group Finafinau, said in a statement Thursday.\n“We are here at Moana Rising because our people deserve more than being heard and ignored. They deserve a meaningful say in decisions that will affect generations. Ocean rights are human rights, and our home is not for sale.”\nThe auction advances President Donald Trump’s April 2025 directive to accelerate access to critical minerals on the ocean floor and reduce reliance on foreign supplies.\nLegal challenges\nThree lawsuits have challenged the process, including one that produced Monday’s ruling.\nJudge Jill A. Otake of the federal district court in Hawaii on Nov. 16 invalidated a National Marine Fisheries Service consultation that had failed to consider the full effects of exploration and mining on protected marine life. Her order did not expressly bar the auction.\nThe Marine Minerals Administration told the Associated Press it had begun renewed consultations with the fisheries service after the ruling, but declined to postpone the auction.\nThe sale took place at the Ventura County Fire Department headquarters in Thousand Oaks, Calif., where several dozen protesters gathered outside.\n2024 moratorium\nAmerican Samoa imposed a moratorium on deep-sea mining in its territorial waters in 2024. The auction areas lie in federal waters beyond that jurisdiction. Territorial leaders and village councils also opposed federal leasing plans, while most of the more than 76,000 public comments submitted to the Bureau of Ocean Energy Management objected to offshore mining.\nIn a lawsuit filed Oct. 19, a council of Samoan chiefs, known as matai, and community groups represented by Earthjustice challenged the federal government’s authority to lease seabed areas off the territory.\nThe plaintiffs argue that the Outer Continental Shelf Lands Act does not authorize mineral leasing off U.S. territories and that the sale violates historic treaty requirements concerning American Samoa.\nA separate lawsuit filed Nov. 18 by Finafinau and the Natural Resources Defense Council seeks to invalidate any leases issued through the auction.\n“We will use the full extent of the law to vigorously push for the leases to be voided,” Earthjustice attorney Chris Eaton said in a statement.\nEarthjustice also represented Fa’asao Amerika Samoa and the Conservation Council of Hawaii in the Endangered Species Act lawsuit filed Aug. 18 that led to Monday’s ruling.\nNext steps\nIf issued, the leases would permit limited preliminary activities and give Wetstone exclusive rights to submit exploration, testing and mining plans. They would not authorize mineral delineation, testing or mining, which require further approvals.\nCommercial deep-sea mining remains at an experimental stage. The sale targets metals found in polymetallic nodules and ferromanganese crusts on the seafloor.\nThe Trump administration is also considering seabed lease auctions near the Northern Mariana Islands, Virginia and Alaska. Separately, the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration is reviewing applications under U.S. law for mining in areas beyond national jurisdiction. Print\nTAG: critical minerals deep sea mining\nCOMMODITY: cobalt Copper manganese Nickel\nREGION: United States\nRepublish this article",
